agent-cli-rust
==============

A terminal-based software engineering agent written in Rust.

The agent works inside one authorized project directory. It explores
the tree, reads and searches source code, edits files, runs development
commands, and reports what it changed after every turn. Plain chat is a
secondary mode.

Version 0.1.0, pre-release. No license file is shipped yet.


STATUS
------

The end-to-end coding loop (explore, modify, build, verify) is covered
by deterministic tests with scripted model responses. Real-model
evaluation and a public release are still open. PROJECT_STATUS.md is the
source of truth for what is and is not implemented.


BUILDING
--------

    cargo build --release
    ./target/release/agent-cli-rust --version

A Rust toolchain with edition 2024 support is required. The project has
no services or database; all state lives in local files.


CONFIGURATION
-------------

Commands that talk to a model take a TOML file as their first argument.
Every field of the [llm] section is optional:

    [llm]
    base_url    = "https://api.openai.com/v1"
    api_key     = ""
    model       = "gpt-4.1"
    temperature = 0.7

If api_key is empty, the environment variable AGENT_CLI_LLM_API_KEY is
used. model must not be blank and temperature must be within 0.0-2.0. A
still-empty key is rejected before any request is sent.

Config.toml in the working tree is a local file. Keep real credentials
out of the repository.

    agent-cli-rust read-config Config.toml

prints the base URL, model and temperature, and reports only whether a
key is set.


SYNOPSIS
--------

    agent-cli-rust claw <CONFIG_PATH> [PROMPT] [OPTIONS]

Runs one coding task. If PROMPT is omitted it is read from stdin until
EOF; blank input is rejected.

    agent-cli-rust claw Config.toml "fix the failing test in src/lib.rs"

    agent-cli-rust claw Config.toml --interactive --workspace ../project \
        --allow-write --allow-exec


OPTIONS
-------

    --workspace <DIR>
        Project directory exposed to the tools. It must exist and is
        canonicalized at startup. Default: current directory.

    --allow-write
        Expose create_file and replace_text. Off by default.

    --allow-exec
        Expose run_command. Off by default. Independent of
        --allow-write, because an authorized command can write files by
        itself.

    --max-iterations <N>
        Maximum model turns per request. Default: unlimited.

    --max-tool-calls <N>
        Maximum tool calls per request. Default: unlimited. A batch that
        would exceed the budget is rejected as a whole.

    --max-context-bytes <N>
        Context budget in bytes of internal message JSON, not tokens.
        Default: 262144 (256 KiB).

    --interactive
        Multi-turn mode, one message per terminal line. See SESSIONS.

    --session-file <PATH>
        Write completed turns to a new archive file. Requires
        --interactive, refuses to overwrite, and the parent directory
        must exist.

    --resume <PATH>
        Load an existing archive and keep updating it. Requires
        --interactive and conflicts with --session-file.

Permissions and budgets always come from the current invocation. They
are not stored in an archive and are not restored from one.


TOOLS
-----

Read-only tools are always available inside the workspace:

    list_files <path>
        Direct children of a directory, workspace-relative, sorted. At
        most 200 entries plus a truncation flag. Symlinks are listed but
        not followed.

    read_file <path> [start_line] [end_line]
        A UTF-8 regular file of at most 1 MiB. Lines are 1-based and
        inclusive; the default is 200 lines from line 1. At most 500
        lines and 32 KiB of content are returned, with a truncation
        flag.

    search_code <pattern> <path>
        Case-sensitive literal search of one file or a directory tree.
        Skips symlinks, .git, target, binary and non-UTF-8 files, and
        files above 1 MiB. Bounded by 5000 directory entries, 8 MiB
        scanned, 100 matching lines, 1 KiB per excerpt and 32 KiB of
        JSON. Does not apply .gitignore rules.

Write tools, enabled by --allow-write:

    create_file <path> <content>
        Creates a file of at most 1 MiB. Never overwrites an existing
        target and never creates parent directories.

    replace_text <path> <edits | old_text + new_text>
        Applies 1-100 exact replacements in order. Each old_text must
        occur exactly once in the content produced by the previous edit.
        Edits are prepared in memory and saved once, only if every edit
        applies. The file is at most 1 MiB before and after. Returns a
        bounded diff. Does not create or delete files.

Exec tool, enabled by --allow-exec:

    run_command <program> <args> [cwd] [timeout_seconds]
        Runs a program with an argument vector, not through a shell,
        inside the workspace. The default timeout is 60 seconds and the
        allowed range is 1-300. stdin is closed; stdout and stderr are
        drained concurrently and each keeps the first 32 KiB. Nonzero
        exits and timeouts are recoverable tool failures returned to the
        model.

Child processes are started without AGENT_CLI_LLM_API_KEY and
OPENAI_API_KEY. On Unix a command runs in its own process group, which
is killed on timeout or cancellation; descendants that leave the group
can escape.


PERMISSIONS AND SAFETY
----------------------

Workspace checks reject paths outside the project root, including
symlinks that resolve outside it. They are boundary checks, not a
sandbox. There is no OS isolation, no per-command approval and no
command filter. --allow-exec lets the model run arbitrary programs with
your user privileges, so grant it only in repositories you trust.

Model-provided tool arguments are untrusted input. File tool output is
bounded, command output is bounded per stream, and every change and
command is listed after the turn so you can inspect it.


SESSIONS
--------

--interactive keeps one conversation until /exit, EOF or Ctrl+C. Blank
lines are ignored. After every completed turn the conversation is
written to:

    <workspace>/.agent-cli/sessions/<unix_ms>-<random>.json

Empty conversations are not written. Startup never resumes a previous
conversation automatically. Archives are limited to 16 MiB; new files
use mode 0600 on Unix and the directory uses 0700. The sessions
directory is listed in .gitignore. An archive can contain prompts,
source code and tool output, so do not publish one.

Interactive commands:

    /sessions      List up to 20 archives for this workspace.
    /resume <n>    Load entry n from that listing and continue in it.
    /new           Start a new conversation and a new archive.
    /exit          Leave.

/resume accepts only an entry from the most recent /sessions listing. It
validates the archive version (1-3) and that the saved workspace is the
current one. A failed resume keeps the current conversation. Restored
history is context only: old tool calls are not replayed, and the
current flags decide what may run now.

--session-file is explicit and refuses to overwrite. --resume updates
the file it loaded and keeps the last good snapshot if a request fails.


CONTEXT BUDGET
--------------

--max-context-bytes limits the internal message JSON sent to the model.
It is not a token budget. Before each request the oldest complete turns
are dropped from the model context while the full history stays on
disk, and a notice is printed. An input that exceeds the budget by
itself is not sent. A turn whose tool output pushes the context over the
budget ends the session instead of starting another request.


EXECUTION REPORT
----------------

After each successful turn the CLI prints the final answer, then:

    Changed files   created files and per-file edit counts.
    Commands        program, arguments and exit code, timeout or signal;
                    each line is capped at 160 characters.
    Tool calls      per-tool counts, total calls and loop count.

Effects are recorded only when a write succeeded or a process started,
so failed calls are not reported as changes. File writes performed
inside run_command are invisible, and effects are not persisted: the
report is a summary, not an audit log. Failed or cancelled turns print
no report, and completed steps are not rolled back.


OTHER COMMANDS
--------------

    chat <CONFIG_PATH> [PROMPT]
        Single-turn model call without tools and without a session.
        PROMPT is read from stdin when omitted.

    fetch <URL> [--max-chars N]
        GET a URL with a 60-second timeout and print the first N
        characters (default 200). Non-2xx responses are errors.

    read-config <PATH>
        Load and validate a configuration file without printing the key.

    --version, --help
        Version (top level only) and command help.


EXIT STATUS
-----------

    0     Success.
    1     Any error. Details go to stderr as "error: ..." followed by a
          "caused by" chain.
    130   Interrupted by Ctrl+C.

Recoverable tool failures (missing file, nonzero exit, timeout, invalid
arguments) do not abort the run; they are returned to the model and may
be corrected on the next turn. Fatal errors end the request, and an
interactive session cannot be reused after one.

Transient model failures are retried before they become fatal: HTTP 408,
429, 500, 502, 503 and 504, connection failures, timeouts and interrupted
bodies. The default is 3 attempts in total, waiting 1 s and then 2 s; a
provider Retry-After in seconds replaces that wait but is capped at 30 s,
and each retry is announced on stderr. Client errors (400, 401, 403,
404), invalid responses and exhausted retries fail immediately with the
attempt count in the message.


FILES
-----

    src/main.rs                 entry point, dispatch, error output
    src/cli.rs                  command definitions and argument checks
    src/config.rs               TOML loading, defaults, validation
    src/agent.rs                runtime loop, messages, budgets, events
    src/openai_compatible.rs    protocol DTOs, tool schemas, HTTP
    src/session.rs              archive format, listing, atomic save
    src/workspace.rs            root resolution and boundary checks
    src/tools.rs                tool dispatch and read-only tools
    src/tools/edit.rs           replace_text planning and saving
    src/tools/create.rs         create_file without clobbering
    src/tools/command.rs        process spawn, output limits, timeouts
    src/tools/search.rs         bounded literal tree search
    src/commands/claw.rs        agent CLI, interactive loop, reports
    src/commands/chat.rs        single-turn chat
    src/commands/fetch.rs       HTTP fetch
    src/commands/read_config.rs configuration summary

    tests/                      unit and integration tests
    SESSIONS.md                 detailed CLI and session guide
    PROJECT_STATUS.md           verified progress and known gaps
    LEARNING_ROADMAP.md         engineering roadmap
    journal/                    development log


TESTING
-------

    cargo test
    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
    cargo fmt --check

The suite is deterministic: model responses come from Wiremock, and no
test needs a real API key, network access or provider availability.
Current total: 217 tests.


LIMITATIONS
-----------

    - Model responses are not streamed.
    - Context control is byte-based; there is no token accounting,
      automatic summary or compaction.
    - No OS sandbox, per-command approval or command allowlist.
    - No overall task time limit and no cancellation of a blocking tool;
      the limits are Ctrl+C, command timeouts and iteration budgets.
    - Effects and traces are not persisted, so a run cannot be replayed.
    - search_code ignores .gitignore and may return an incomplete subset.
    - Session archives are checked before replacement; a concurrent
      writer can still race with that check.
    - chat remains a minimal no-tool entry point.


DOCUMENTATION
-------------

    SESSIONS.md          Full CLI and session reference.
    PROJECT_STATUS.md    Completed work, open work, technical debt.
    LEARNING_ROADMAP.md  Phases 0-8 and their acceptance criteria.
    journal/             Session-by-session engineering log.
